[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SOLID in Microservices and Distributed Systems"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Applying SOLID principles to distributed systems and microservices requires shifting our perspective from class-level design to service-level architecture, network boundaries, and inter-service communication."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) in Microservices"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In object-oriented programming, SRP states that a class should have only one reason to change. In distributed systems, this translates to the concept of bounded contexts and microservices having a single business capability."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A service should encapsulate a single business domain (e.g., Inventory Management vs. Payment Processing)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Changes to the billing logic should never require redeploying or modifying the shipping service."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Open/Closed Principle (OCP) via API Versioning and Event-Driven Architecture"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Services should be open for extension but closed for modification. In distributed architectures, this is achieved by publishing events and versioning APIs rather than altering existing contracts."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Example of backward-compatible event publishing in JSON
{
  "eventId": "evt_109283",
  "version": "2.0",
  "data": {
    "userId": "usr_555",
    "newField": "value"
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) and Contract Testing"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"LSP ensures that subclasses can be substituted for their base classes without breaking behavior. In microservices, this applies to service providers and API consumers. If Service B replaces Service A, it must honor the exact same API contract."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use consumer-driven contract testing (e.g., Pact) to guarantee that service replacements do not violate expected outputs."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) in API Design"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Fat interfaces force clients to depend on methods they do not use. In distributed systems, this manifests as monolithic APIs returning excessive payload data that various frontend or downstream consumers do not need."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adopt technologies like GraphQL or BFF (Backend For Frontend) patterns to allow clients to request only the data they require."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) and Infrastructure Decoupling"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. In microservices, business logic should not be tightly coupled to specific databases, message brokers, or third-party HTTP clients."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"// Python pseudo-code demonstrating DIP with ports and adapters
class OrderProcessor:
    def __init__(self, message_broker: MessageBrokerInterface):
        self.broker = message_broker

    def process_order(self, order):
        # Business logic here
        self.broker.publish("order.created", order)"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"By adhering to SOLID principles at scale, engineering teams can build resilient, loosely coupled distributed systems that scale independently and withstand frequent iterations."}]
